I'll start by reading the story and the code context so the change stays limited to what PER-383 story 02 asks for.
The story is store and admin wishlist API routes. I'll follow existing Medusa route patterns and the Story 1 service instead of the BMAD workflow.
Store routes treat customer auth as optional, and admin routes already require a user. I'll follow those conventions and wire the wishlist handlers to the existing service.
The wishlist tests failed before they reached the routes: Postgres rejected the connection because the client password was not a string. I'll check how this repo supplies database credentials.
Store and admin wishlist endpoints are in place and covered by the HTTP tests.

Customers manage their own list at `POST` and `GET /store/wishlist` and `DELETE /store/wishlist/:product_id`. Those routes require a customer session or bearer token and take the customer id from `req.auth_context.actor_id`. Adding the same product again returns the existing row with status 200. An empty list is `{ wishlist_items: [], count: 0 }`.

Admins use `GET /admin/customers/:customer_id/wishlist` and `DELETE /admin/customers/:customer_id/wishlist/:product_id`. Admin routes already require a user (session, bearer, or API key). These two also check `customer:read` and `customer:update`.

